WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.530
Evidence, economics and community seed leadership.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:09.620
This is the video for module 3 of Agricultural Hybridization and Crop Improvement.

3
00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:16.710
Your trial tells you how much more a new variety yields. It does not yet tell you whether it pays.

4
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:22.440
Good seed is also wasted if it dies or weevils eat it before planting.

5
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.230
You will learn to run a community seed bank with honest records.

6
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.470
And you will turn your trial into a short report and a 90-day plan.

7
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:52.010
A partial budget lists only what changes when you switch. For example, Rukayat's hybrid gave 923 kg per hectare more than her usual seed. At about 600 naira per kg, that is 553,800 naira of added income.

8
00:00:53.260 --> 00:01:03.970
Now the added costs. Hybrid seed costs 100,000 naira, minus her usual saved seed worth 14,000, so 86,000 naira extra.

9
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.400
The hybrid needs about one extra bag of fertiliser, roughly 40,000 naira.

10
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:17.580
Harvesting, shelling, bags and transport for the extra grain add about 15,000 naira.

11
00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.560
Her total added costs are 141,000 naira.

12
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:37.300
Net benefit is the added income minus the added costs. For Rukayat, 553,800 minus 141,000 is 412,800 naira.

13
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:51.920
The marginal rate of return is net benefit divided by the extra money spent. Here it is about 293%. Farming is risky, so aim for 50% to 100% or more.

14
00:01:53.170 --> 00:02:05.570
Break-even is added costs divided by the grain price. 141,000 divided by 600 is 235 kg. Below that gain, she loses money.

15
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:14.240
If the price drops to 400 naira at harvest, her net benefit is still 228,200 naira.

16
00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.610
But if a dry spell cuts the gain to 200 kg, she loses money that year.

17
00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.040
Use the price you will actually receive, not the highest price you have heard.

18
00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:33.950
Seed is alive, and heat and moisture make it age fast. Dry it on a clean tarpaulin or raised mat.

19
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.250
Avoid very hot zinc roofing or concrete at midday, which can damage the seed.

20
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:50.930
Then do the salt jar test. Put a handful of seed and two or three spoons of dry salt in a dry glass jar. Shake it and wait about 15 minutes.

21
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.430
If salt sticks to the jar, the seed is still too damp. Dry it longer and test again.

22
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:04.930
Store it in airtight bags such as PICS bags, which starve weevils of oxygen. Label each one.

23
00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:14.080
If you use a storage insecticide, use only an approved product at the label dose, and keep planting seed apart from food grain.

24
00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:27.830
In a community seed bank, members borrow seed at planting and return more after harvest. For example, a women's group in Benue State starts with 60 kg of certified cowpea seed.

25
00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.490
Twelve members borrow 4 kg each.

26
00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:38.070
After harvest, ten members return double, 8 kg each. That is 80 kg.

27
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:45.060
Two members lost their crops to flooding. The group records 8 kg as owed next season.

28
00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:54.860
The stock grows to 92 kg. Every returned lot gets a germination test, and one poor lot is kept for food, not seed.

29
00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:07.660
Write your decision rules before you see the results. For example, Hauwa in Kano adopts only if a variety wins in all three blocks and the return is above 100%.

30
00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:15.190
Variety A meets every rule. But she plants it on half her land, not all, because one season is limited evidence.

31
00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:27.960
Variety B won on average but not in every block, and its return was only 45%. She re-tests it on a small area, and keeps her usual seed as insurance.

32
00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:38.370
Write a short, honest report. Give your goal and the varieties, your methods and layout, and season notes like dry spells or animal damage.

33
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:46.230
Put the results in a table with corrected yields, then your partial budget and your decision. Say plainly what did not work.

34
00:04:47.480 --> 00:05:01.210
To remember. A partial budget lists only what changes. Test a lower price and a bad season. Dry seed and check it with the salt test, then store it airtight. And set your rules before you see the results.

35
00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:13.790
Now build your partial budget with local prices, do the salt jar test, and draft seed bank rules with a ledger. Then write your report outline and your dated 90-day plan.

36
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.080
Continue with the lessons below.
